            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for which, then the Latin for and not."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: nūper",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nūper — \"recently\". Latin's past tense, the perfect, has its own endings: vīdī — \"I saw\", vēnī — \"I came\". Nūper librum lēgī. — \"I recently read a book.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nūper is usually explained as novum, \"new\", with -per, \"through\", the ending of semper, \"always\" (Lewis & Short): newly, of late."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
