          "title": "You'll want to know: anteā",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "anteā — \"before, previously\". It is ante, \"before\", with eā. Anteā Rōmae habitāvī. — \"Before, I lived in Rome.\" habitāvī is the perfect of habitō."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "anteā is ante, \"before\", with eā, \"that\" (Lewis & Short), like posteā, \"afterwards\", and proptereā, \"because of that\". ante is usually traced to Proto-Indo-European h2enti, \"facing, in front\" (EtymDB 2.1)."
            }
          ]
        },
